"""
Murallas de Ávila (Q3050235), the medieval walls of Ávila, Spain — built by code.

Author: Álvaro C. Chulvi (with AI assistance). Licence of the resulting model: CC BY 4.0.
The walls: Romanesque granite walls of the 11th–12th century (traditionally begun in 1090 after the repopulation
of the town by Raymond of Burgundy), finished in the 12th century and altered up to the 14th; restored in the
19th and 20th centuries. No known architect. Public domain.

What is modelled: the whole circuit (about 2.5 km) with its semicircular towers (cubos), the crenellated parapet
of the wall walk, the nine gates as dark arched passages, the two great gates of the east front (Puerta del
Alcázar and Puerta de San Vicente: two tall towers joined high up by an arch), and the cimorro — the apse of the
cathedral built into the east wall — as a massive two-tier crenellated drum. Not modelled: the cathedral itself,
the houses and palaces built against the wall, the Espadaña del Carmen, stairs and railings of the wall walk.

Measurements (see meta.json for the sources):
- OpenStreetMap way 45976728 (historic=citywalls, height=12): a closed outline of the wall itself, 1022 nodes —
  the inner face of the wall (nodes 1–146), the outer face with every tower drawn as a bump (nodes 147–1021, 0),
  open at the cimorro, where the cathedral (way 50694497) takes its place. The wall is 2.4–4.3 m thick in the
  outline (3 m on average according to the literature). Towers are found as the runs of outer nodes more than
  5 m from the inner face: 84 towers here, plus the cimorro (es.wikipedia counts 87 cubos): big D-shaped cubos 6–7.5 m wide
  projecting 8–10 m on the north, west and east fronts, shallow half-towers 3–5 m wide on the south front
  (as on the photos of the Paseo del Rastro).
- Gates: the pedestrian/road ways tagged tunnel=building_passage that cross the wall (Alcázar, Peso de la Harina,
  San Vicente, Carmen, Puente, Mariscal, Rastro, San Isidro/Malaventura, la Santa).
- Ground: EU-DEM 25 m (Copernicus, via OpenTopoData) sampled every 10 m along the wall, 10 m inside, on the wall
  and 19 m outside. y = 0 is the ground at the origin (1119.8 m); the ground at the foot of the wall goes from
  +16 m (east, by the cathedral) down to −51 m (west, above the Adaja). The wall therefore follows the terrain:
  wall walk 9.8 m above the outside ground (smoothed over 50 m; es.wikipedia: 2515 m long, 12 m high, 3 m thick), solid parapet 1.0 m, merlons 1.15 m with
  pyramidal caps 0.45 m (top of the merlons ≈ 12.4 m above the ground: es.wikipedia gives 12 m on average);
  foundations 6 m below the lowest ground near each point.
- Heights of the towers and gates measured on photos (Wikimedia Commons, people and cars for scale, ±10 %):
  cubos rise 2.4 m above the wall walk (shallow south towers 1.2 m); Puerta del Alcázar and Puerta de San
  Vicente: arch springing 3 m above the wall walk, towers about 20 m high. Cimorro (outline of the cathedral apse,
  way 50694497): machicolation on corbels at 11.4 m, first crenellated parapet at 15.5 m, upper drum (3.2 m set
  back) to 20.5 m, from photos (±15 %).
Warm golden-grey granite for the walls; the cimorro is greyer.

Axes (docs/MODEL_SPEC.md): +Y up, the east front (Puerta del Alcázar – cimorro – Puerta de San Vicente) faces +Z
(heading 90.9°), +X to the north; origin on the ground at the centroid of the walled town.

    python model_sources/Q3050235_murallas_de_avila.py   -> models/Q3050235/model.glb (+ footprint in meta.json)
"""
